  "name": "Question 7",
  "problem": "The matrix $T$ is given below. Let ${t}_{1}$, ${t}_{2}$, and ${t}_{3}$ be the respective columns of $T.$\nDetermine if $B=\\text{{}{t}_{1},{t}_{2}\\text{}}$ is a basis of $\\text{Col}(T).$ \nIf that is the case, find ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}$, where ${t}_{3}={x}_{1}{t}_{1}+{x}_{2}{t}_{2}.$\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 3 & 4 \\\\ 2 & -6 & 4\\end{aligned}]\n$$",
  "worked_solution": "First, we reduce the matrix $T$ to reduced row echelon form (RREF):\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 3 & 4 \\\\ 2 & -6 & 4\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :=(-1){R}_{1} \\\\  & \u223c[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 & -4 \\\\ 2 & -6 & 4\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & \u223c[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 12\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :=\\frac{1}{12}{R}_{2} \\\\  & \u223c[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the matrix above, we see that the pivot columns are the $1$st and $3$rd columns. So, a basis of $\\text{Col}(T)$ is ${{t}_{1},{t}_{3}}.$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $\\text{{}{t}_{1},{t}_{2}\\text{}}$ is not a basis of $\\text{Col}(T).$",
